xt":"Advanced"},{"type":"flow","items":["Computational Materials","Materials Informatics","Digital Materials Discovery"],"sep":"+"},{"type":"h","text":"Target progression"},{"type":"flow","items":["Year 1","Materials science + crystallography + ceramic fundamentals","Year 2","Ceramic processing + phase diagrams + sintering","Year 3","XRD + SEM + materials testing + research internship","Year 4","Advanced materials + modelling + AI/data + research project"]},{"type":"h","text":"First job\nMaterials Engineer / R&D Engineer / Ceramic Engineer"},{"type":"h","text":"Postgraduate specialization\nM.Tech/M.S. Materials Science / Advanced Materials"},{"type":"h","text":"3–7 years\nMaterials Scientist / R&D Scientist"},{"type":"h","text":"7–12 years\nSenior Materials Scientist"},{"type":"h","text":"12–20+ years\nPrincipal Scientist / R&D Lead"},{"type":"h","text":"Best overall positioning"},{"type":"flow","items":["Ceramic Engineering","Materials Science","Characterization","Advanced Materials","Computational/AI Methods"],"sep":"+"},{"type":"p","text":"For this specific career, postgraduate specialization is particularly valuable: a B.Tech provides the engineering and materials-processing foundation, while an M.Tech/M.S./Ph.D. can provide the deeper research expertise normally expected for senior Materials Scientist positions."}]}]}